Ways the change could fail a criterion (what each rating is measured against):

  observe-test-behavior
    [missing_case] observe-test-behavior/empty-request-returns-no-observation
      An empty test-id list returns `SandboxRunResult(outcomes=[])`, so the review run does not execute any selected tests or observe any behavior for that covered input.
      src/acceptance/execution/sandbox.py#0
    [scope_too_narrow] observe-test-behavior/duplicate-test-ids-are-deduplicated
      `run_tests` collapses repeated test ids with `dict.fromkeys`, so a named set containing duplicates is not run or observed as requested and the result cannot reflect each requested occurrence.
      src/acceptance/execution/sandbox.py#0
    [not_wired] observe-test-behavior/report-file-path-not-created-in-plugin-env
      The plugin reads `ACCEPTANCE_SANDBOX_REPORT`, but the subprocess environment only sets `PYTHONPATH` to the plugin directory and never wires the copied plugin module path into pytest's import path in a way that guarantees the plugin is loaded from the sandboxed copy; if pytest imports a different module or fails to load the copied one, no actual test behavior is observed through this path.
      src/acceptance/execution/sandbox.py#0
      src/acceptance/execution/netblock.py#0
    [wrong_output_shape] observe-test-behavior/skipped-tests-recorded-as-not-started
      Skipped tests are reported as `not_started` rather than as a distinct observed outcome, so the captured result does not preserve what the test actually did when it ran and called `pytest.skip()`.
      src/acceptance/execution/netblock.py#0
      src/acceptance/execution/outcome.py#0

  isolated-sandbox
    [other] isolated-sandbox/network-block-only-covers-socket-layer
      The sandbox only patches Python-level socket entry points, so code under test can still reach the network through other mechanisms such as C extensions or libraries that bypass `socket`, meaning the run is not fully isolated.
      src/acceptance/execution/netblock.py#0
    [other] isolated-sandbox/user-site-packages-can-leak-in
      The subprocess environment disables `PYTHONNOUSERSITE`, but it still inherits the interpreter's default site configuration and any already-installed user/site packages reachable through the chosen interpreter, so the run can execute with launch-side code outside the intended sandbox boundary.
      src/acceptance/execution/sandbox.py#0
    [other] isolated-sandbox/local-socket-traffic-remains-allowed
      The network block explicitly allows `AF_UNIX` sockets, so code under test can still communicate through local socket endpoints; if the criterion expects an isolated sandbox with no reachable external services, this leaves a communication channel inside the run.
      src/acceptance/execution/netblock.py#0

  network-access-blocked-for-tests
    [scope_too_narrow] network-access-blocked-for-tests/loopback-connections-allowed
      The sandbox only blocks socket families outside `_ALLOWED_FAMILIES`, and `_ALLOWED_FAMILIES` includes `AF_UNIX`; a test that reaches a network service over loopback or another allowed family can still make a connection instead of being blocked.
      src/acceptance/execution/netblock.py#0
    [other] network-access-blocked-for-tests/python-socket-patching-misses-other-entry-points
      The block is only installed by monkeypatching Python-level `socket` functions; code that reaches the network through another path, such as a C extension or a preexisting socket object, can still connect because those entry points are not intercepted.
      src/acceptance/execution/netblock.py#0

  no-launch-credentials-visible  (reused from an earlier run)
    [scope_too_narrow] no-launch-credentials-visible/environment-allowlist-leaks-unlisted-credentials
      The subprocess environment is built from a small allowlist, but it still inherits whatever launch-machine values happen to be in the named variables. If a credential is stored in one of those allowed names, it remains visible to the code under test.
      src/acceptance/execution/sandbox.py#0
    [other] no-launch-credentials-visible/usersite-packages-still-visible
      The sandbox only sets `PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1` and trims `PYTHONPATH`; it does not otherwise prevent the launched interpreter from loading launch-machine code or configuration from its normal site-packages and startup paths, so credentials exposed through those paths could still be visible to the code under test.
      src/acceptance/execution/sandbox.py#0

  clean-timeout-stop
    [scope_too_narrow] clean-timeout-stop/per-test-timeout-not-armed-on-some-platforms
      The per-test alarm is only armed when signal.setitimer and SIGALRM are available; on platforms without them, the sandbox refuses to run instead of stopping an exhausted time budget cleanly, so the criterion is not met for those environments.
      src/acceptance/execution/netblock.py#0
    [other] clean-timeout-stop/whole-run-timeout-leaves-processes-running
      When the whole-run budget expires, the code sends SIGTERM/SIGKILL to the process group but does not verify that every child has actually exited before returning, so a run could be reported as stopped while subprocesses continue executing briefly or remain alive.
      src/acceptance/execution/sandbox.py#0

